New Great Lakes PFAS Transport Research provides site-specific data for Ontario and Quebec practitioners.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:49.580
Flood declarations in Manitoba and Quebec highlight immediate needs for updated risk mapping on

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:56.680
active files. If you only note one thing today for your compliance calendar, review project eligibility

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:02.480
lists for overlap with current contaminated sites or remediation mandates in Alberta and

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:08.920
Saskatchewan, while monitoring Canada Gazette Part 1 for 30-day comment windows opening this month.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:16.180
The federal government announced $26 million in funding for 17 clean energy, grid modernization,

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:22.440
and energy security projects in Alberta and Saskatchewan. The allocation supports infrastructure

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:30.240
upgrades that intersect with existing environmental compliance obligations under provincial EPA and EMPA

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:36.900
frameworks. Prior federal programs of this type have required recipients to integrate contaminated

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:43.220
site assessments and spill prevention upgrades into funded work. Practitioners managing portfolios

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:49.240
in these provinces should map funded sites against current phase 2 or risk assessment files to

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:55.880
identify new data sharing requirements. Implementation timelines remain unspecified in the announcement,

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:02.380
but project lists are now public. Alberta and Saskatchewan firms should confirm whether their active

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:09.300
mandates qualify for supplementary federal reporting streams. The weekly Canada Gazette Part 1 issue

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:14.680
from July 4th was published with the standard complement of proposed regulations and notices.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:20.760
No specific environmental instruments are detailed in the index, but the addition triggers the

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:26.960
routine 30-day comment cycle for any new CPA or Fisheries Act proposals contained within.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:34.260
The $26 million package targets grid and clean energy projects without explicit new regulatory

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:41.920
text. Recipients must still satisfy existing provincial contaminated sites reporting under EPEA

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:49.480
and EMPA, when ground disturbance occurs. Transport modeling shows PFAS migration from water

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:55.940
column to drinking water intakes and human serum in the Great Lakes Basin. Finding supply sites

00:02:55.940 --> 00:03:04.380
specific attenuation data usable in Ontario and Quebec risk assessments under OREG 153 over 0.4

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:11.080
and equivalent provincial protocols. Two-class action filings allege inadequate municipal flood risk

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:17.300
mitigation following June 20th rainfall in the Montreal area. Remediation and environmental

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:23.740
consultants working on West Island sites should verify whether current closure reports address updated

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:30.660
2026 flood frequency assumptions. Manitoba communities issued emergency declarations as a

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:37.640
synabolin river levels rise toward a July 12th peak. Field teams should confirm groundwater monitoring

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:43.960
schedules account for elevated water tables before resuming sampling. If you're working on contaminated

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:50.460
sites files in Alberta or Saskatchewan, review federal clean energy project lists for new assessment

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:57.160
triggers. If you're working on active Quebec LQE site files, cross-check West Island flood class

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:03.120
action filings for updated risk assumptions. If you're working on Manitoba groundwater monitoring,

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:08.760
update schedules to capture post-peak water table conditions after July 12th.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:14.740
If you're working on Saskatchewan projects, confirm whether any funded initiatives intersect existing

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:22.140
EMPA reporting obligations. Mark your calendar for July 12th when the a synabolin river flood peak is

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:28.880
forecast. Mark your calendar for mid-July when Canada Gazette comment windows are expected to open on

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:35.400
any part one proposals from the July 4th issue. Mark your calendar for ongoing monitoring of

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:41.880
Great Lakes PFAS transport data releases for incorporation into Ontario and Quebec risk assessments

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:48.240
this quarter. A closure package for a former service station in southern Manitoba shows benzine

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:53.840
concentrations stable in three wells but rising sharply in a fourth after spring flooding,

00:04:53.840 --> 00:05:00.320
despite no new release. The difference arises because the fourth well sits inside the active flood

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:06.800
plane where the water table now intersects a residual LNAPL smear zone that was previously above

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:13.260
the seasonal high. Under Manitoba Environment Act guidance and CCME Tier 1 protocols,

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:18.980
practitioners must distinguish between plume migration and temporary hydraulic connection.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:24.700
The latter often produces transient exceedances that resolve once water levels drop.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:30.920
Experienced assessors cross-reference well hydrographs against river stage data from the nearest

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:37.500
gauge rather than relying solely on quarterly snapshots. The most common mistake is assuming any

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:43.200
post flood increase signals a new source and triggering unnecessary additional investigation.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:49.320
The fix is to collect one extra round of water level and product thickness measurements during the

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:56.700
next low water period before revising the conceptual site model. Tomorrow watch for updates on any new

